msgstr ""

#: src/introduction.md:31
msgid ""
"**New to EW:** the tutorial [A first schematic page with "
"potentials](tutorials/first-page.md), then [A terminal strip and a "
"cable](tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md) and [Reports and "
"PDF](tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md). If you come from QElectroTech, "
"[Importing a QElectroTech project](tutorials/import-qet.md) brings a project "
"of yours over."
msgstr ""

#: src/introduction.md:35
msgid ""
"**A task at hand:** the how-to guides, among them [Choose "
"parts](how-to/choose-parts.md), [Draw a panel "
"layout](how-to/panel-layout.md) and [Upgrade a "
"project](how-to/upgrade-a-project.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/introduction.md:37
msgid ""
"**A fact to look up:** the [rule reference](reference/rules/index.md), with "
"a page per check rule; the window's [tools](reference/window/tools.md), "
"[dialogs](reference/window/dialogs.md) and "
"[panels](reference/window/panels.md); and the [release "
"notes](reference/release-notes/index.md) of every release."
msgstr ""

#: src/introduction.md:40
msgid ""
"**Coming from another program:** [EPLAN and EW](explanation/eplan-and-ew.md) "
"and [QElectroTech and EW](explanation/qet-and-ew.md) relate the concepts you "
"know to EW's."
msgstr ""

#: src/introduction.md:43
msgid "What the guide does not contain"
msgstr ""

#: src/introduction.md:45
msgid ""
"The design documents, the architecture decision records and the work plan "
"stay in the repository, next to the code they describe. This guide links to "
"them; it does not copy them. Start from the [documentation "
"map](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/README.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/introduction.md:49
msgid "Offline use"
msgstr ""

#: src/introduction.md:51
msgid ""
"The guide builds without network access and is meant to be bundled with the "
"application, so that the help works without a connection. Pages that link to "
"the repository need a connection to follow those links."
msgstr ""

#: src/introduction.md:54
msgid "Sample data"
msgstr ""

#: src/introduction.md:56
msgid ""
"Every example uses the anonymised golden project (`golden/J-E-2416`, licence "
"CC0-1.0). It holds placeholders where a real project would name a customer, "
"an end user or a person, such as `<customer>` and `<drawing id>`. Do not put "
"real customer data into examples that you contribute to this guide."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/index.md:3
msgid ""
"A tutorial is a lesson. It takes you through one complete task, with every "
"step spelled out and a visible result at the end, so that you learn how the "
"parts of EW fit together by using them. You do not need to understand why a "
"step works to do it; the [explanation](../explanation/index.md) section and "
"the [reference](../reference/index.md) say why and what exactly."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/index.md:8
msgid ""
"Each tutorial runs as a test of EW's own build, so its steps, menu paths and "
"keys are the ones the program has."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/index.md:10 src/how-to/index.md:6
msgid "Available now"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/index.md:12
msgid ""
"[A first schematic page with potentials](first-page.md): a new project, a "
"transformer and two lamps placed and wired, the potentials named, the "
"Potentials navigator and the Messages pane read. You need EW installed and "
"an empty folder."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/index.md:15
msgid ""
"[A terminal strip and a cable](terminal-strip-and-cable.md): on the project "
"of the first tutorial, a terminal strip placed and wired on both sides, a "
"cable created from its part with the field wires as its cores, the strip "
"editor and the Cables navigator read. You need the project you saved at the "
"end of the first tutorial."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/index.md:18
msgid ""
"[Reports and PDF](reports-and-pdf.md): on a copy of the sample project, its "
"report pages read, a parts list and a terminal overview added, the project "
"exported as one PDF and the print preview read. You need EW installed and an "
"empty folder."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/index.md:21
msgid ""
"[Importing a QElectroTech project](import-qet.md): a small QElectroTech "
"project imported, its migration report read, its missing wire drawn and its "
"parts chosen, until the report holds only the record of the import. You need "
"EW installed and the tutorial's file "
"[lamp-circuit.qet](files/lamp-circuit.qet)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/index.md:24
msgid ""
"[Draw a six-pin power block](symbol-editor.md): a power block drawn in the "
"symbol editor, checked on the connection grid, its eight orientations "
"previewed, saved to a library and tested on a new page. You need EW "
"installed and an empty folder."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/index.md:28
msgid ""
"The `ew` command-line program has a how-to of its own: [Use the command line "
"on the golden project](../how-to/headless-workflow.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/index.md:31
msgid "Planned"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/index.md:33
msgid ""
"The documentation requirements (NFR-DOC-03) name the tutorials that follow "
"at version 1.0: a PLC card with addressing, and 2D/3D layout with the wire "
"list."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you draw your first schematic page in the ElektraWatt "
"window: a small control transformer that feeds two cabinet lamps. You place "
"the symbols, draw the wires, give the two potentials their names and read "
"what EW knows about the circuit. At the end you have a saved project whose "
"checks show no error."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:7 src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:8
#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:7 src/tutorials/import-qet.md:8
#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:8 src/how-to/install.md:7
#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:7 src/how-to/choose-parts.md:7
msgid "What you need"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:9 src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:9
#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:10 src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:10
msgid ""
"ElektraWatt installed and started; see [Install "
"ElektraWatt](../how-to/install.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:10
msgid "An empty folder for the project, or a place where EW may create one."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:12
msgid "Nothing else: the project starts from EW's built-in basic project."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:14 src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:13
#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:12 src/tutorials/import-qet.md:16
#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:13 src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:12
msgid "Steps"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:16
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ New project…** (`Ctrl+N`) and pick an empty folder named "
"`first-page.ew`. EW creates the project from the basic project and shows its "
"one empty page, folio 1, with its frame."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:18
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** (`I`). In the symbol picker, choose "
"**transformer-1ph** and click near the top left of the page. Then choose "
"**Draw ▸ Select** to put the tool away. The transformer -1T1 is on the page, "
"with its primary pins 1U1 and 2U1 at the top and its secondary pins 1U2 and "
"2U2 at the bottom."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:22
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** again, choose **lamp**, and click twice, "
"to the right of and below the transformer, to place two lamps side by side. "
"Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The lamps -1E1 and -1E2 are on the page, each with "
"the pins X1 at the top and X2 at the bottom."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:25
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Wire** (`W`). Click the transformer's pin 1U2, "
"then the pin X1 of -1E1: the wire is drawn. Draw three more wires the same "
"way: from X1 of -1E1 to X1 of -1E2, from 2U2 to X2 of -1E1, and from X2 of "
"\\-1E1 to X2 of -1E2. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The two lamps are wired in "
"parallel to the transformer's secondary. Each wire takes a number, such as "
"1.1."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:29
msgid ""
"Click the wire between the two lamps' X1 pins, then choose **Draw ▸ Connect "
"▸ Define potential…**. Type the name `24VAC`, the type `L` and the voltage "
"`24 V AC`, and confirm the form. Every wire of that potential now carries "
"the name 24VAC."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:32
msgid ""
"Click the wire between the two lamps' X2 pins, choose **Draw ▸ Connect ▸ "
"Define potential…**, and give it the name `0VAC`, the type `N` and the "
"voltage `24 V AC`. The circuit has its two named potentials."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:35
msgid ""
"Choose **View ▸ Panels ▸ Navigators ▸ Potentials**. The Potentials navigator "
"lists 0VAC and 24VAC, each with its two wires."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:37
msgid ""
"Choose **Check ▸ Messages**. The Messages header shows E 0, W 0 and pending "
"5: five findings wait on the page you are drawing until you leave it or run "
"the checks. They are three devices without a part (EW-CORE-041: -1E1, -1E2 "
"and -1T1) and the transformer's two open primary pins (EW-POT-009: -1T1:1U1 "
"and -1T1:2U1)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:41
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ Save** (`Ctrl+S`). The title bar no longer marks the project "
"as changed, and the status bar says Saved."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:44 src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:49
#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:38 src/tutorials/import-qet.md:56
#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:42
msgid "What you learnt"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:46
msgid ""
"A project starts from a basic project; **File ▸ New project…** makes one in "
"a folder of its own."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:47
msgid ""
"A symbol you place is a function of a device, and the device gets its tag "
"(-1T1, -1E1) from the numbering scheme."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:48
msgid ""
"Wires join connection points; the wires that touch form a potential, which "
"you name with Define potential…."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/first-page.md:49
msgid ""
"The checks run while you draw. A warning tells you what is still open; the "
"[rule reference](../reference/rules/index.md) explains each rule, and **Help "
"▸ Documentation** on a finding opens its rule's page."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you take the lamp circuit of the first tutorial out of the "
"cabinet: a third lamp sits on the machine, and it is fed through a terminal "
"strip in the cabinet and a cable. You place the strip's terminals, wire "
"their cabinet side and their field side, create the cable with its cores, "
"and read the strip in the strip editor and the cable in the Cables "
"navigator. At the end you have a saved project whose checks show no error."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:10
msgid ""
"The project of the tutorial [A first schematic page with "
"potentials](first-page.md), `first-page.ew`, as you saved it at its end."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:15
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ Open…** (`Ctrl+O`) and open `first-page.ew`. Its page shows "
"the transformer -1T1 and the lamps -1E1 and -1E2."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:17
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Terminal strip**. EW creates an empty terminal "
"strip. It takes its tag when its first terminal is placed."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:19
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** (`I`) and choose **terminal**. In the list "
"of strips, choose the new strip. Click twice, to the right of -1E2 and level "
"with it, to place two terminals side by side. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The "
"terminals -1X1:1 and -1X1:2 are on the page. Each has its cabinet side, "
"clamp a, at the bottom and its field side, clamp b, at the top."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:23
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** again, choose **lamp**, and click above "
"and to the right of the terminals. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The lamp -1E3, "
"the lamp on the machine, is on the page."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:26
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Wire** (`W`). Draw the cabinet side: from X1 of "
"-1E2 to the bottom of -1X1:1, and from X2 of -1E2 to the bottom of -1X1:2. "
"Then the field side: from the top of -1X1:1 to X1 of -1E3, and from the top "
"of \\-1X1:2 to X2 of -1E3. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The terminal -1X1:1 "
"carries 24VAC and -1X1:2 carries 0VAC to the lamp on the machine."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:30
msgid ""
"Click the field wire of -1X1:1, then Shift+click the field wire of -1X1:2. "
"Choose **Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Cable…** (**Cable** on the Draw tab). The form "
"proposes the tag -1W1 and lists the two wires with the core each gets. Type "
"`3G1.5` as the **Type**, a three-core cable of 1.5 mm² whose last core is "
"green-yellow: the first wire gets the core BN, the second BU. Choose "
"**Apply**. The toast says \"Cable -1W1 made from 2 wires\": the cable is "
"made with its three cores and the two field wires are its cores, in one step "
"that one Undo takes back. The cable takes its page number from its first "
"core, and the wires take the cores' 1.5 mm² and colours."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:37
msgid ""
"Choose **View ▸ Panels ▸ Navigators ▸ Cables** and click the cable -1W1. In "
"the Properties pane, set **Length** to `5 m`. The Cables navigator lists the "
"cable -1W1 with its three cores: BN, BU and GNYE."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:40
msgid ""
"Click the terminal -1X1:1 and choose **Edit ▸ Strip editor**. The Terminal "
"strips navigator shows the strip -1X1 as a table: terminal 1 on 24VAC, with "
"-1E2:X1 on side 1 and the field wire to -1E3:X1 via -1W1 BN on side 2; "
"terminal 2 on 0VAC, with -1E2:X2 and -1E3:X2 via -1W1 BU."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:43
msgid ""
"Choose **Check ▸ Run checks** (`F7`). The toast says \"Check: 0 errors, 8 "
"warnings, 2 infos\". The warnings are the devices that have no part yet and "
"the transformer's open primary pins; the infos say that no report covers the "
"strip or the cable yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:46
#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:35 src/tutorials/import-qet.md:53
#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:39
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ Save** (`Ctrl+S`). The title bar no longer marks the project "
"as changed."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:51
msgid ""
"A terminal strip is a device of its own, and each terminal you place from "
"the strip's list is one of its terminals, with a cabinet side and a field "
"side."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:53
msgid ""
"**Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Cable…** makes the selected wires a cable's cores in one "
"form: the cable's type as you write it (`3G1.5`, `4x0.75`, or a cable part's "
"number) gives the cores, and each wire's core can be changed before Apply. "
"The same command on a cable's wire opens that cable, to add a wire or take "
"one out. A cable without a drawn cable line takes its page number from its "
"first core."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:57
msgid ""
"The strip editor and the Cables navigator show what passes through each "
"terminal and each core, from the drawing."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md:58
msgid ""
"The reports that list them, the terminal diagram and the cable list, are the "
"subject of [Reports and PDF](reports-and-pdf.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you work on a copy of the sample project, a complete "
"control-cabinet project. You read its report pages, add two more, write the "
"whole project as one PDF and look through the print preview. Report pages "
"are not drawn: EW generates them from the schematic, so they follow every "
"change."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:10
msgid "An empty folder, or a place where EW may create one."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:14
msgid ""
"Choose **Help ▸ Tutorials ▸ Reports and PDF…** and name a new folder "
"`reports.ew`. EW makes a fresh copy of the sample project there and opens "
"it; the banner says \"A fresh copy of the sample project was made for the "
"tutorials.\" This tutorial's page of the guide opens beside it."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:17
msgid ""
"Read the report pages in the Pages navigator, in section 9 (folios 900 to "
"999). They are marked generated: the parts list (Partlist, 901 and 901.1), a "
"terminal diagram for each terminal strip (903 to 905), the cable overview "
"and diagrams (906), the wire list, the PLC overview, the potential overview, "
"the device tag overview and the revision overview. Click one, such as 904, "
"to see it."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:21
msgid ""
"Choose **Output ▸ Report block…**. In the form, choose the report **Parts "
"list**, keep or type the first folio `912`, type the title `Parts list`, and "
"choose **Create**. The toast says \"Added the report block Parts list from "
"folio 912\". The Pages navigator lists the new pages 912 to 912.3: the parts "
"list device by device, where the Partlist on 901 sums the parts up."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:25
msgid ""
"Choose **Output ▸ Report block…** again, choose **Terminal overview**, the "
"first folio `913` and the title `Terminal overview`, and choose **Create**. "
"The page 913 lists every terminal strip of the project on one page."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:28
msgid ""
"Choose **Output ▸ Export PDF…** (`Ctrl+Shift+E`) and save the file as "
"`reports.pdf`. When the export is done, the toast says \"Exported "
"reports.pdf: 13 drawing pages, 3 section divider pages and 25 report "
"pages\", with the file's size. Open the file from the toast to see it: one "
"PDF/A-2b file with every page, its outline and its cross-reference links."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:32
msgid ""
"Choose **Output ▸ Print…** (`Ctrl+P`). The Print dialog shows the range, the "
"paper and a preview of the first page: \"1 of 41\". Step to the next page in "
"the preview to see page 2. Choose **Cancel** to close the dialog without "
"printing."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:40
msgid ""
"A report block generates report pages from the project: a parts list, "
"terminal diagrams, a cable list and more. **Output ▸ Report block…** adds "
"one, from the first folio you give."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:42
msgid ""
"Report pages follow the drawing: change a device, a terminal or a cable, and "
"its report pages change with it."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:43
msgid ""
"**Output ▸ Export PDF…** writes every page into one PDF; **Output ▸ Print…** "
"shows each page before it prints."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md:44
msgid ""
"[A terminal strip and a cable](terminal-strip-and-cable.md) shows where the "
"terminal diagrams and the cable list take their rows from."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you bring a small QElectroTech project into ElektraWatt: a "
"24 V supply that feeds a lamp through a switch. You import it, read the "
"migration report that says what the import changed, guessed or left out, and "
"fix the findings that are yours to fix, until the report shows only the "
"record of the import itself. At the end you have a saved EW project."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:11
msgid ""
"The tutorial's QElectroTech project, "
"[lamp-circuit.qet](files/lamp-circuit.qet). Save it in a folder of your own."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:13
msgid ""
"The project has one folio with four elements: the supply G1, the switch S1, "
"a lamp without a label and an earth symbol wired to the supply's PE "
"terminal. One conductor is missing on purpose: the lamp's return to the "
"supply."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:18
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ Import…**, pick `lamp-circuit.qet`, and name the new "
"project's folder `lamp-circuit.ew`. The import's card says what it will "
"make: \"Create project makes a new project in the folder lamp-circuit.ew, "
"with 1 page, 4 devices and 3 connections.\" Its verdict follows: \"The file "
"was imported. 7 points are worth a look, and none stops you from working.\" "
"Under **Worth a look** and **For your information** it lists the kinds of "
"findings, each with its count. Nothing is written yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:24
msgid ""
"Choose **Create project**. EW creates the project and opens it. The folio "
"looks as it did in QElectroTech: the frame and the title block, the labels "
"S1 and G1, and the three conductors where QElectroTech drew them. The "
"Messages pane shows the **Migration report**, chosen in its header beside "
"the project's own scheme: no errors, 7 warnings and 7 infos."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:28
msgid ""
"Read the report, rule by rule. Some findings are the record of the import, "
"and stay:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:29
msgid ""
"EW-IMP-119: the supply's element has no meaning EW knows, so G1 came in as a "
"generic black box."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:30
msgid ""
"EW-IMP-103 and EW-NUM-001: the labels S1 and G1 are kept as frozen tags, "
"because they do not follow the project's numbering."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:32
msgid ""
"EW-IMP-122: the lamp and the earth had no label. EW gave them the tags -1A1 "
"and -1X1, and the page prints no tag for them, as QElectroTech printed none."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:34
msgid "EW-IMP-117: the file uses QElectroTech's default cross-reference formula."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:35
msgid "EW-IMP-006: the file's font, Sans Serif, is printed in Noto Sans."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:37
msgid ""
"The others are live checks of the project, and yours to fix: EW-POT-009 says "
"that the lamp's pin X2 and the supply's pin - are not connected; and "
"EW-CORE-041 says that none of the four devices has a part."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:39
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Wire** (`W`). Click the lamp's pin X2, at its "
"bottom, then the supply's pin -, at the bottom of G1: the return wire is "
"drawn. Choose **Draw ▸ Select**. The two EW-POT-009 warnings are gone. While "
"you edit, the Messages pane shows the project's own scheme, drafting."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:42
msgid ""
"Click the lamp and choose **Edit ▸ Choose part…**. Type `lamp`, choose "
"**EW-GEN-LMP-1**, and confirm with **Choose**. The lamp has its part."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:45
msgid ""
"Click the switch S1, choose **Edit ▸ Choose part…**, type `on/off` and "
"choose **EW-GEN-SW-1**."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:46
msgid ""
"Click the supply G1, choose **Edit ▸ Choose part…**, type `power supply` and "
"choose **EW-GEN-PSU-1**."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:47
msgid ""
"Click the earth, choose **Edit ▸ Choose part…**, type `earth` and choose "
"**EW-GEN-TRM-2**. Every device has its part, and EW-CORE-041 is gone."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:49
msgid ""
"Choose **Check ▸ Run checks** (`F7`). The Messages pane shows the migration "
"report again, with no error, 1 warning (EW-IMP-119) and 7 infos (EW-IMP-006, "
"EW-IMP-103 twice, EW-IMP-117, EW-IMP-122 and EW-NUM-001 twice): the record "
"of the import, nothing left to fix."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:58
msgid ""
"**File ▸ Import…** says what an import will make before it writes anything, "
"and makes a new EW project of its own; the QElectroTech file is not changed."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:60
msgid ""
"The folios arrive as QElectroTech drew them: the wires on their paths, the "
"texts, shapes and images, the frame and the title block."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:62
msgid ""
"The migration report lists two kinds of finding: the import's own record "
"(the EW-IMP rules, and the frozen tags), which stays as a note of what "
"changed, and the live checks of the project, which go away when you fix them."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:64
msgid ""
"In the Messages pane's header you switch between the migration report and "
"the project's own scheme."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/import-qet.md:65
msgid ""
"[QElectroTech and EW](../explanation/qet-and-ew.md) explains how "
"QElectroTech's elements, labels, conductors and folio reports become EW's "
"functions, devices, wires and interruption points."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:3
msgid ""
"In this tutorial you draw a reusable symbol: a power block with six "
"connections, designated 1 to 5 and FE. You check it on the connection grid, "
"preview its eight orientations, save it to a library, keep a portable copy "
"of the draft and test the symbol on a new page of a project. At the end you "
"have a library with the symbol and a saved project with a test copy, ready "
"to wire."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:11
msgid "An empty folder."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:15
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ New project…** (`Ctrl+N`) and pick an empty folder named "
"`symbol-scratch.ew`."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:16
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol editor…**. Type **Power block** as its "
"**Name**, and in **Library** type the key `example.power`. The editor takes "
"the place of the page, with its own command bar."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:19
msgid ""
"In **Drawing**, choose **Rectangle** and draw the body from `(4, 0)` to "
"`(24, 28)` mm: click both corners, or type them under **Draw by "
"coordinates** and choose **Add**."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:21
msgid ""
"Choose **Connection point**, set its direction to **Facing left**, and add "
"six connection points at x = 0 mm and y = 4, 8, 12, 16, 20 and 24 mm, with "
"the designations 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and FE. Choose **Line** and draw a short lead "
"from each connection point to the body's left edge, at x = 4 mm."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:24
msgid ""
"Choose **Text slot**. Add a **Pin designation** field beside each connection "
"point, with that point's pin number, and a **Device tag** field above the "
"body. The validation says **Ready to save**. FE is a printed designation: "
"before you use the symbol, give the FE pin its electrical role under "
"**Electrical data**, **Function definition**, and never confuse it with PE."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:28
msgid ""
"Click B to H under **Variants**: the large preview shows the symbol turned "
"and mirrored, every pin keeping its number and turning its direction with "
"the drawing. Choose A to edit again."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:30
msgid ""
"In **Electrical data**, under **Connection points**, change the first "
"point's x to 4.5 mm. The validation says \"Connection point 1 is off the "
"connection grid. Move it onto the grid before saving.\" Press `Ctrl+Z` to "
"undo the change. Fine drawing coordinates, such as 0.5 mm, remain valid."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:33
msgid ""
"In **Library**, type `symbols.ewlib` as the **Library folder** and choose "
"**Save to library**: a name without a folder is a library in your user "
"library folder. A message says where the symbol was saved."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:35
msgid ""
"Under **Open or import**, type `power.symbol.json` as the **Symbol file** "
"and choose **Export draft**; it is written beside the library. The file is "
"the portable draft, with the identity the save gave it."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:37
msgid ""
"Choose **Test on a new page**. The editor makes way for a new page with a "
"test copy of the symbol selected, ready to wire."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:44
msgid ""
"The symbol editor keeps its own draft and undo history; saving it to a "
"library does not change placed copies. A changed symbol is saved as the next "
"version, 1.0.1 after 1.0.0."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:46
msgid ""
"Connection points must lie on the 4 mm connection grid; the editor refuses "
"to save a point off it, and says which."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:47
msgid ""
"**Close editor** keeps the draft and its undo history: **Draw ▸ Place ▸ "
"Symbol editor…** returns to it. In a later session, open `power.symbol.json` "
"under **Open or import**: its next save updates the same symbol."
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:50
msgid ""
"The same workflow is available to AI tools and repeatable scripts in "
"[`symbol-editor.ewui`](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/book/tutorial-scripts/symbol-editor.ewui). "
"Run it in a fresh directory:"
msgstr ""

#: src/tutorials/symbol-editor.md:58
msgid ""
"The script creates the project beside itself and the library and "
"`power.symbol.json` in your user library folder, and reopens the draft. See "
"the [reference](../reference/symbol-editor.md) for JSON, actions and import "
"compatibility."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:3
msgid ""
"A how-to guide helps you reach one goal that you already understand: short "
"steps, no background. If you are new to EW, start with a "
"[tutorial](../tutorials/index.md) instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:8
msgid ""
"[Install ElektraWatt](install.md): the Windows setup, silent installs, the "
"Linux AppImage, the CLI archives and `EW_CONTENT_DIR`, upgrading and "
"uninstalling."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:10
msgid ""
"[Use the application window](application-window.md): the start page, new, "
"sample and imported projects, recovery, the command palette, workspaces, the "
"first-run setup, the company-standard wizard and F1."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:12
msgid ""
"[Draw a page from the text interface](text-interface.md): reading a page as "
"text, placing, autoconnect, frozen tags, terminal strips, external points, "
"wires and junctions, delete and export, without a screen."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:14
msgid ""
"[Use the command line on the golden project](headless-workflow.md): the `ew` "
"program checks a project, writes its pages as PDF and SVG, exports the cable "
"list and the bill of materials, and queries it in SQL. You need a checkout "
"of the repository and the `ew` program."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:17
msgid ""
"[Define a supply potential](define-supply-potential.md): a potential's name, "
"type and voltage from a selected wire, junction or terminal."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:19
msgid ""
"[Choose parts](choose-parts.md): a device's part from the property grid or "
"the part selection, a device placed from its part, and where the parts are "
"listed."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:21
msgid ""
"[Draw a panel layout](panel-layout.md): a panel layout page, its enclosure "
"and mounting plate, rails and ducts, and the devices placed on them."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:23
msgid ""
"[Upgrade a project](upgrade-a-project.md): what a release reads and writes, "
"opening a project of an older format, the backup of the first save, `ew "
"upgrade`, and what changes in the format from 0.1.0."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:26
msgid ""
"More follow the [command-line how-to](headless-workflow.md), for example "
"replaying a command log with `ew apply`, checking a project in a CI job, and "
"exporting for a single page range."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:29
msgid "Writing a how-to guide"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/index.md:31
msgid ""
"A guide names its goal in the title (\"Export one page range as SVG\"), "
"lists what you need, gives numbered steps and ends when the goal is met. "
"Facts that belong to a command or a rule go to the "
"[reference](../reference/index.md), and are linked, not repeated."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide installs ElektraWatt on Windows with its setup or on Linux as an "
"AppImage, or the command-line program `ew` alone from its archive on Windows "
"or Linux, and then upgrades or removes it. Each download carries the version "
"in its name, shown here as `<version>`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:9
msgid ""
"For the setup: Windows 10 or 11 on an x86-64 PC. No administrator rights and "
"no network connection are needed."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:10
msgid ""
"For the AppImage: Linux with glibc 2.35 or newer (as on Ubuntu 22.04 and "
"Debian 12) on x86-64. No administrator rights, no network connection and no "
"other package are needed."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:12
msgid ""
"For a CLI archive: Windows or Linux (glibc 2.35 or newer, as on Ubuntu 22.04 "
"and Debian 12) on x86-64."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:14
msgid "Verify a download"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:16
msgid ""
"Every release lists the SHA-256 checksum of each of its files in "
"`SHA256SUMS`, one of the release's files. Check a download before you run it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:19
msgid ""
"Linux: put the downloaded files and `SHA256SUMS` in one folder and run, in "
"that folder,"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:25
msgid ""
"It prints `OK` after the name of each file you downloaded. Any other word "
"means the file is not the released one: delete it and download it again."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:27
msgid ""
"Windows (PowerShell): `Get-FileHash <file> -Algorithm SHA256` prints the "
"file's checksum. It must be the one that `SHA256SUMS` lists before the "
"file's name (PowerShell prints it in capitals)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:30
msgid ""
"The releases are not signed yet; this check shows that a file is the one the "
"project published."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:32
msgid "Install with the setup on Windows"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:34
msgid "Run `ElektraWatt-<version>-x64-user-setup.exe`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:35
msgid ""
"The setup is not signed yet, so Windows may stop it with **Windows protected "
"your PC**. Choose **More info**, check that the file is the one you "
"downloaded, then choose **Run anyway**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:37
msgid ""
"Keep the folder the setup proposes, `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\ElektraWatt`, "
"or choose another one of your own, then choose **Install**. The folder must "
"be new or empty, hold both `elektrawatt.exe` and `uninstall.exe` of an "
"earlier installation, or be the folder of the installed version. For any "
"other folder that holds files, **Install** stays off, so that removing "
"ElektraWatt later never deletes them: choose another folder, or, when it is "
"an earlier installation that lost one of those files, delete that folder and "
"run the setup again."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:42
msgid ""
"On the last page, leave **Run ElektraWatt** ticked to start it, and choose "
"**Finish**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:44
msgid ""
"The setup installs for you alone: the program, the command-line program "
"`ew.exe`, the starter content, the sample project and this guide, all in the "
"one folder, with a shortcut **ElektraWatt** in the Start menu. It does not "
"change `PATH`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:48
msgid ""
"To install without questions, for example from a script, run the setup with "
"`/S`. Add `/D=<folder>` as the last argument, without quotes even when the "
"folder has spaces, to install into another folder:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:55
msgid ""
"The same folders are refused here too: the setup installs nothing and ends "
"with exit code 2. Give another folder, or delete the old one and run the "
"setup again."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:58
msgid "Run the AppImage on Linux"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:60
msgid ""
"The AppImage is one file, `ElektraWatt-<version>-x86_64.AppImage`, that "
"holds the program, the command-line program `ew`, the starter content, the "
"sample project and this guide. It installs nothing: you run the file itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:63
msgid "Make it executable, then start it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:70
msgid ""
"The AppImage opens itself through FUSE, which most desktops have. Where FUSE "
"is missing (in a container, or on a system without the `fuse` kernel "
"module), it stops with a message about FUSE. Run it with "
"`--appimage-extract-and-run` as the first argument instead: it unpacks "
"itself into a temporary folder for that run."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:78
msgid ""
"To run `ew`, give `ew` as the first argument, then its own arguments (after "
"`--appimage-extract-and-run` where FUSE is missing):"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:86
msgid ""
"Like the `ew` of a CLI archive, it reads the starter content only through "
"`--content` or `EW_CONTENT_DIR`. The AppImage's own content sits in a folder "
"that changes with each run, so for now unpack the AppImage once, which "
"writes the folder `squashfs-root` where you run it, and point "
"`EW_CONTENT_DIR` at the content in it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:90
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"./ElektraWatt-<version>-x86_64.AppImage --appimage-extract\n"
"export EW_CONTENT_DIR=\"$PWD/squashfs-root/usr/lib/elektrawatt/content\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:95
msgid "Unpack it again after an upgrade."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:97
msgid ""
"Keep the file where you like, for example in `~/Applications`. The AppImage "
"is not signed yet: check that the file is the one you downloaded against the "
"checksums published with its release."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:100
msgid "Use the CLI archive"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:102
msgid ""
"The archives hold `ew` with the starter content and the sample project, for "
"a machine without the window or for a CI job: "
"`ew-<version>-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.zip` and "
"`ew-<version>-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:105
msgid "Unpack the archive. It holds one folder, `ew-<version>-<target>`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:106
msgid ""
"`ew` finds the starter content through `--content` or the environment "
"variable `EW_CONTENT_DIR`, not beside itself. Set `EW_CONTENT_DIR` to the "
"folder's `content` folder:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:108
msgid "Linux: `export EW_CONTENT_DIR=<folder>/content`"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:109
msgid "Windows (PowerShell): `$env:EW_CONTENT_DIR = \"<folder>\\content\"`"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:110
msgid ""
"Check it: `ew --version` prints the version, and `ew export pdf "
"<folder>/samples/J-E-2416.ew --out sample.pdf` writes the sample project's "
"drawings as a PDF."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:113
msgid ""
"The `ew.exe` that the setup installs finds the starter content the same way: "
"set `EW_CONTENT_DIR` to the `content` folder of the installation."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:116
msgid "Upgrade"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:118
msgid ""
"Run the setup of the newer version as above. It finds the installed version "
"and proposes its folder, removes it first with its own uninstaller, then "
"installs, so no file of the older version remains. Your settings and keymaps "
"stay as they are."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:122
msgid ""
"To upgrade the AppImage, download the newer file, make it executable and "
"delete the older one. Your settings and keymaps stay as they are."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:125
msgid ""
"To upgrade `ew` from an archive, unpack the newer archive and point "
"`EW_CONTENT_DIR` at its `content` folder."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:127
msgid "Uninstall"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:129
msgid ""
"Open **Settings**, then **Apps** and **Installed apps** (on Windows 10: "
"**Apps & features**)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:130
msgid "Find **ElektraWatt** and choose **Uninstall**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:132
msgid ""
"To uninstall without questions, run `uninstall.exe /S` from the installation "
"folder. The uninstall removes the installation folder, the Start-menu "
"shortcut and the entry in the list of apps. It leaves your projects, your "
"settings and keymaps (in `%APPDATA%\\ElektraWatt`), and your libraries and "
"the program's cache (in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\ElektraWatt`): delete those folders "
"yourself when you no longer want them."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:137
msgid ""
"The AppImage installs nothing: delete its file. It leaves your projects, "
"your settings and keymaps (in `~/.config/elektrawatt`), your libraries and "
"the program's cache (in `~/.local/share/elektrawatt` and "
"`~/.cache/elektrawatt`), and the program's lock and journal files (in "
"`~/.local/state/elektrawatt`): delete those folders yourself when you no "
"longer want them."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/install.md:142
msgid "A CLI archive installs nothing: delete its folder."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide describes the desktop application `elektrawatt`: what is on the "
"start page, how to make or open a project, how to arrange the window, and "
"where F1 leads. Every action of the window is an action of one registry, so "
"the same names work in the menu, in the command palette (`Ctrl+K`) and in a "
"headless UI script (`elektrawatt --headless --script`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:8
msgid "Start page"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:10
msgid ""
"With no project open the window shows the start page. It has no news feed "
"and opens no connection."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:12
msgid ""
"**New project** makes a project in a new, empty directory from your company "
"basic project, or from the built-in basic project when you have none."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:14
msgid ""
"**Open...** opens a project directory; the recent projects are listed below "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:15
msgid ""
"**Open sample project** opens the CC0 sample project read-only; use **Save "
"as...** to make your own copy."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:16
msgid ""
"**Import QElectroTech project** reads a `.qet` file into a new project "
"directory."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:17
msgid ""
"**Create company standard** starts the [company-standard "
"wizard](#company-standard-wizard)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:18
msgid "**Tutorials** opens the tutorials and a fresh copy of the sample project."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:19
msgid ""
"The quick switches set the language and the keymap preset. The privacy line "
"says that this version has no update check; **change** opens the privacy "
"step of the [first-run setup](#first-run-setup)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:22
msgid "Project view"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:24
msgid ""
"With a project open the window shows the page list, the page view and the "
"status bar. The page view shows the current page read-only. `Page Down` and "
"`Page Up` move between pages, `Ctrl+G` goes to a folio, `Ctrl+Z` and "
"`Ctrl+Shift+Z` undo and redo, `Ctrl+S` saves and `Ctrl+Shift+S` saves under "
"a new name."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:28
msgid "New project"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:30
msgid ""
"**File, New project** (`Ctrl+N`) asks for a new, empty directory and writes "
"the project there at once. The project starts from the default basic project "
"(a company basic project made by the wizard, or the built-in one) with new "
"identities for every record."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:34
msgid "Sample project"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:36
msgid ""
"**File, Open sample project** opens the sample project read-only, so that "
"nothing is written into the installation. **Save as...** writes a copy into "
"a directory of your own and continues in the copy."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:39
msgid "Import a QElectroTech project"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:41
msgid ""
"**File, Import...**, beside **Open...**, asks for a `.qet` file and then for "
"a new, empty directory. The importer reads the file into a new project and "
"writes it to the directory; a banner counts the pages and the findings of "
"the migration report. The same entry takes a project archive (`.ewz`): it "
"asks whether to extract it into a new folder and open that, or to open it "
"read-only."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:46
msgid "Unsaved changes and recovery"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:48
msgid ""
"Closing, quitting or opening another project with unsaved changes asks "
"whether to save, discard or cancel. If the application ends while a project "
"has unsaved changes, the next open finds the journal of that session and "
"opens the project read-only with a banner. **File, Discard unsaved changes "
"of the earlier session** deletes the journal after a confirmation and opens "
"the project for editing."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:53
msgid "Command palette"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:55
msgid ""
"`Ctrl+K` (or `Ctrl+Shift+P`) opens the palette: type a part of a name and "
"press `Enter` to run the selected entry. Disabled entries are listed last "
"with the reason. `Escape` closes it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:58
msgid "Keyboard shortcuts"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:60
msgid ""
"**Help, Keyboard shortcuts** lists the bindings of the EW preset. `F1` opens "
"this guide at the page of what has the focus: an open dialog, the palette, "
"the wizard, the project view or the start page. Over a menu item with a "
"shortcut the tooltip ends with \"F1: learn more\"; `F1` while the pointer is "
"on the item opens the page of that item."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:64
msgid "Workspaces"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:66
msgid ""
"The project view is a dock: drag the border between two panes to resize "
"them, and drag a pane's title onto another pane to rearrange or tab it. The "
"built-in workspace is **Schematic**. **View, Workspace** lists the saved "
"workspaces, saves the current layout under a name and resets the layout. A "
"workspace is a file `workspaces/<name>.json` in the configuration directory; "
"the layout is saved a second after its last change."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:71
msgid "First-run setup"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:73
msgid ""
"On the first start the application asks for the language and what you come "
"from (EPLAN, QElectroTech, KiCad or other), says what it does with your data "
"(this version has no update check and sends no crash reports; **Privacy "
"(PRIVACY.md)** opens the full statement), asks for the appearance (theme and "
"scale), and then what to do first. Every step can be skipped. **Help, "
"First-run setup** runs it again. Nothing in it makes a network connection."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:78
msgid "Company-standard wizard"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:80
msgid ""
"The wizard collects the conventions of your company and writes a **basic "
"project**, from which new projects start. It has ten steps: the company, the "
"frame and title block, the colour profile, the size profile, identifiers and "
"numbering, structure and sections, revisions, languages and formats, reports "
"and checks, and a review. Each step says what it is and where you can change "
"it later, and its **Result** shows what your choices make, such as the name "
"of the third contactor on page 102; the **Example** beside it shows a cover "
"page and a schematic page with your choices. Your choices are kept in the "
"configuration directory until you finish, and the basic project is written "
"to a directory you choose and becomes the default for **New project**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:88
msgid "About"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/application-window.md:90
msgid ""
"**Help, About ElektraWatt** shows the version, the page-view renderer, the "
"privacy state, the licence, the engineering-verification disclaimer, the "
"support line (before 1.0 only the latest 0.x release is supported), and the "
"third-party notices."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide is for drawing without a screen: a headless UI script "
"(`elektrawatt --headless --script`), or any client that reads the interface "
"as text (`ui.describe`) and the content of a page as text (`page.describe`). "
"Every step below is an action of the same registry as the window's menus; "
"the parameters are those the action list prints."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:7
msgid "Read the page"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:9
msgid ""
"`page.describe` (with `folio`, or the page shown) lists the page in reading "
"order: a `rep` line per drawn symbol with its tag, cell, symbol and "
"position, its `cp` lines (connection points, the wires drawn to each, the "
"potential), a `wire` line per drawn connection and a `junction` line per "
"junction."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:13
msgid ""
"**Positions are the positions you click.** Every `at <x> <y> mm` is a point "
"of the sheet, the same coordinates `canvas.press`, `canvas.pointer` and "
"`cursor.set` take: `cursor.set` with a `cp` line's position puts the grid "
"cursor on that connection point, and the cursor line names it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:16
msgid ""
"**A `wire` line ends with a point on the wire** (`at <x> <y> mm`) where a "
"click with the Select tool picks that wire and nothing else. A wire whose "
"drawn route runs along another wire or through a symbol for its whole length "
"has no such point; select it by its ends instead: `select.set` with `refs` "
"`[\"wire:-101F1:1 -> F55:1\"]` (the ends as the `wire` line prints them, "
"either way round; a junction end is `junction <n>`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:21
msgid "Place symbols"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:23
msgid ""
"`place.pick` (or Insert, Symbol... and the picker) arms the Place tool; "
"`nav.goto` with a cell (`101-B2`) or `cursor.set` moves the ghost, and "
"`canvas.activate` (Enter) places it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:25
msgid ""
"**Autoconnect.** When the ghost's free connection points face free "
"connection points on the page, the ghost proposes wires to them: "
"`ui.describe` prints them as `canvas proposals <n> \"<cp>\" -> "
"\"<target>\"`, and the status hint says that Enter connects them. "
"`canvas.activate` with `alt` (Alt+Enter, Alt+Click) places the symbol "
"without connecting; `tool.next_proposal` (Tab) tries the next target along "
"each ray. After a placement that connected, the toast names the wires it "
"drew; one Undo removes the symbol with its wires."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:31
msgid "Freeze a tag"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:33
msgid ""
"Select the device (`select.set` with `refs` `[\"tag:-101S1\"]`) and set its "
"frozen tag as the page should print it: `props.set` with `row` "
"`device/tag.frozen` and `value` `S51` prints `S51`; `-S51` prints `-S51` "
"(the sign shown). An empty value unfreezes it. The derived tag row's Freeze "
"action and the `tag.frozen.text` and `tag.frozen.sign` rows do the same in "
"several steps."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:38 src/reference/window/panels.md:92
msgid "Terminal strips"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:40
msgid ""
"`strip.new` creates an empty terminal strip; the toast names it. It draws "
"nothing until its first terminal is placed: choose the terminal symbol in "
"the symbol picker, and a second picker lists the strips (`row strip "
"\"<tag>\" terminal \"<next>\" @<n>`); choose the strip there. The terminal "
"takes the row's proposed designation unless the choice names one "
"(`picker.choose` with `designation` `PE`, on either picker). A strip without "
"terminals prints its tag with question marks in it (`-X??`) because its "
"counter is given with its first terminal; the `@n` at the end of its row "
"selects it (`select.set` with `ids` `[\"@n\"]`), and `edit.delete_selection` "
"deletes a strip that has no terminals. After its first terminal the strip "
"has its real tag (`-X1011`); `place.pick` with a strip name the project does "
"not have fails with a message that lists the project's strips."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:49
msgid ""
"In the Terminal strips navigator a row is named by what it prints: a "
"terminal by its tag, the strip's label, a colon and its own (`-X1031:1`), "
"and a strip by its tag (`-X1031`). `navigator.select` with `keys` and "
"`table.set` with `rows` take these names (`table.set` with `kind` `strips`, "
"`rows` `[\"-X1031:1\",\"-X1031:2\"]`, a `column` and a `value`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:53
msgid ""
"A terminal's connection points print `strip:terminal/clamp` (`-X1031:1/b`): "
"the starter terminal has clamp `a` on side 1 (internal) and `b` on side 2 "
"(field), `1X1:1/a` and `1X1:1/b`. Where two clamps of one level carry the "
"same letter, as on a library terminal that names them so, the label adds "
"`#n` in connection-point order: `-X1:1/a#1` and `-X1:1/a#2`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:57
msgid "Bridge terminals"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:59
msgid ""
"Select two or more terminals of one strip on the page (click the first "
"terminal symbol, Shift+click the others, or `select.set` with their "
"representations), then run `strip.bridge` (Draw ▸ Bridge terminals). It "
"draws a plug-in bridge between each two terminals that follow each other in "
"the strip's order among the selected ones, in one undo step: selecting "
"`-X1031:1` to `:4` draws 1-2, 2-3 and 3-4, whatever order you selected them "
"in. The bridges join the group of a bridge they touch, or else the strip's "
"next free group. A bridge ends on the terminal's bridge shaft when its part "
"has one; a terminal without a part (or with a part that has no shaft) is "
"bridged at its lowest side-1 connection point, so you can draw the bridges "
"before choosing the parts. A pair that is bridged already is left as it is. "
"The command is refused when fewer than two terminals are selected, when they "
"belong to more than one strip, or when every pair is bridged already; the "
"refusal names the reason."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:69
msgid "External points"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:71
msgid ""
"`structure.new` with `kind` `location` and the location (`+J-E-206`), then "
"`external_ref.new` with the location, the device and the connection, once "
"per connection. Each says in the toast what it created; the external "
"reference's label is `<device>:<connection>` (`JE-N1000-03:L`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:74
msgid ""
"With the Interruption point tool armed, `ip.external` takes that label, or "
"the label after the location as the page prints it (`+J-E-206 / "
"JE-N1000-03:L`); the ghost becomes an external point. A text that no "
"reference has fails with a message that lists the labels the project has."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:78
msgid "Draw wires"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:80
msgid ""
"With the Wire tool armed, put the grid cursor on a connection point (its "
"`cp` position), press Enter, move to the other end and press Enter. The "
"`canvas route` line names the start (`from`) and the end (`end`) before you "
"commit."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:83
msgid ""
"`cp.next` and `cp.previous` step the cursor through the page's connection "
"points in reading order of the cell **column by column** (column 0 to 16, "
"and in a column row A to H), then by position; this differs from the page "
"text's order, which goes row by row. Going to a `cp` position with "
"`cursor.set` needs no walk."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:86
msgid ""
"**Ending a wire on a wire** makes a junction there and splits that wire. The "
"route's `end` names the wire it would split by its ends (`@101-C12 F55:2 -> "
"E55:L`): check it before Enter when two wires run through one cell."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:89
msgid "Delete"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:91
msgid ""
"`edit.delete_selection` (Delete) deletes what is selected on the page shown: "
"a representation, a wire, a junction or an item. A device selected by its "
"tag is deleted as its representations on the page shown (the device stays, "
"its functions unplaced); a device that no page draws, such as an empty "
"terminal strip, is deleted itself. A device drawn only on another page is "
"refused (`reason-not-on-page`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:96
msgid "Save and export"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/text-interface.md:98
msgid ""
"`file.save` saves. `file.export_pdf` with a file name writes the PDF; the "
"toast names the file, its page count and its size."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:3
msgid ""
"Here you use the `ew` command-line program on the golden project: you check "
"the project, write its pages as PDF and SVG, export the cable list and the "
"bill of materials, and ask the project questions in SQL. Nothing needs the "
"desktop application, and nothing leaves your machine: `ew` never uses the "
"network."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:7
msgid ""
"The golden project, `J-E-2416`, is an anonymised control-cabinet project "
"that EW uses as its acceptance target. Where a real project names a customer "
"or a person, it holds a placeholder such as `<customer>`. You only read it "
"here; none of the steps changes it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:11
msgid "Before you start"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:13
msgid "You need:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:15
msgid ""
"a checkout of the ElektraWatt repository, which holds the project in "
"`golden/`;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:16
msgid ""
"the `ew` program, built from the checkout with `cargo build --release -p "
"ew`. It is then `target/release/ew` (`target\\release\\ew.exe` on Windows). "
"The examples write plain `ew`; put the program on your `PATH` or type its "
"path."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:20
msgid ""
"Run every command from the root of the checkout. Check that `ew` works; it "
"prints its name and its version:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:26
msgid "Then make a directory for the results:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:32
msgid "Where the project comes from"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:34
msgid ""
"The folder `golden/J-E-2416/tables/` holds the project as plain CSV tables: "
"one file per kind of record (devices, terminals, connections, pages and so "
"on). The importer of the `ew-import-tables` crate turns these tables into "
"project records, and the checked-in project `golden/J-E-2416.ew` is "
"generated from the same tables. The [golden "
"README](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/golden/J-E-2416/README.md) "
"describes the tables and their format."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:40
msgid ""
"**Not built yet.** The importer is a library today. The `ew import csv` "
"subcommand that will run it from the terminal does not exist yet, so this "
"guide starts from the checked-in project. When the subcommand exists, this "
"step will become a command line."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:44
msgid ""
"A project is a directory, here `golden/J-E-2416.ew`. Every `ew` command that "
"works on a project takes it as its first argument."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:47
msgid "Step 1: check the project"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:49
msgid ""
"`ew check` runs the check rules over the project and prints what it finds, "
"one finding per line:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:58
msgid "The first lines are the findings, for example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:64
msgid ""
"Each line gives the severity, the rule ID, the rule's six-digit code, the "
"thing the finding is about and a message. The last line counts the findings, "
"and the standard error stream repeats the count with the scheme's name. The "
"golden project holds deliberate defects, so it reports errors: the cable "
"`-3W1`, for one, has no length, so a cable list cannot be complete."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:69
msgid "Look at the exit code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:76
msgid ""
"`ew` exits with 0 when the project has no unwaived errors, 1 when it has "
"some and 2 when it could not run the check at all (a project that cannot be "
"opened, a wrong option). A script should treat 2 as a failure of the tooling "
"and 1 as an answer about the drawing. The exit codes of every subcommand are "
"in [doc 17 "
"§3.2](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/design/17-extensibility.md#32-exit-codes)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:81
msgid ""
"Each rule has a page in the reference: "
"[`EW-CORE-033`](../reference/rules/ew-core-033.md) is the cable without a "
"length, and [`EW-NUM-001`](../reference/rules/ew-num-001.md) is the "
"information that a frozen device tag differs from what the numbering scheme "
"would give."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:85
msgid ""
"`ew check` takes a scheme and a mode. The default is the `pre-delivery` "
"scheme in `release` mode; `--format json` and `--format md` change the "
"output, and `--out <file>` writes it to a file."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:88
msgid "Step 2: write the pages as PDF and SVG"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:90
msgid ""
"`ew export pdf` writes every page of the project into one PDF/A-2b file, "
"with an outline, page labels and cross-reference links:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:99
msgid ""
"The second line, on the standard error stream, counts remarks about the "
"rendering of single pages and says what they are: here, fields of the title "
"blocks that the project leaves empty. They do not stop the export. The "
"directory of `--out` must exist, which is why you made `out` first."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:103
msgid ""
"`ew export svg` writes one SVG file per page into a directory. Use `--pages` "
"to select folios; this writes the pages numbered 101 to 106 that exist:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:112
msgid ""
"The directory now holds `101.svg`, `102.svg`, `103.svg` and `106.svg`. `ew` "
"replaces the directory as a whole, so it must hold SVG files only; a stale "
"page of an earlier export cannot remain in it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:115
msgid ""
"The same export always gives the same bytes. The only date in the output "
"comes from the project, or from the environment variable `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` "
"when you set it, never from the clock of your machine; `--threads <n>` "
"changes the speed and never the result."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:119
msgid "Step 3: export the cable list and the bill of materials"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:121
msgid "Two more exports write the rows of a report as CSV:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:130
msgid ""
"Open `out/cables.csv` in a text editor or a spreadsheet. It starts like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:132
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"row,Cable,Cable type,Core,Colour,mm²,From,To,Length\n"
"heading,Cable overview,,,,,,,\n"
"data,-3W1,6XV1830-0EH10,,,,,,length missing\n"
"data,-106W1,NEBU-G78W5-K-2-N-LE5,,,,\"-X1031, -X1032\",-201U1,from part\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:139
msgid ""
"The cable `-3W1` says \"length missing\": it is the finding of step 1, seen "
"from the other side. The cables `-106W1` and `-106W2` take their length from "
"their part."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:142
msgid ""
"`--mode` of `ew export bom` is `parts` (the default; every component part) "
"or `assembly`, which replaces the parts a configured product covers by one "
"row for the product. Both write the rows of the project's own report page, "
"so the CSV equals what the drawing prints."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:146
msgid "Step 4: ask the project questions with SQL"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:148
msgid ""
"`ew sql` runs one read-only `SELECT` over the project's SQL views, which are "
"named `api_v1_...`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:150
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ ew sql golden/J-E-2416.ew \"SELECT count(*) FROM api_v1_terminal\"\n"
"count(*)\n"
"28\n"
"1 rows\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:157
msgid ""
"The result is CSV with a header line, on the standard output; the row count "
"goes to the standard error stream. Ask for the cables and where their "
"lengths come from:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:160
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ ew sql golden/J-E-2416.ew \"SELECT tag, cores, length_source FROM "
"api_v1_cable ORDER BY tag\"\n"
"tag,cores,length_source\n"
"-106W1,5,part\n"
"-106W2,5,part\n"
"-3W1,3,missing\n"
"3 rows\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:169
msgid ""
"`ew sql schema` prints every view with its columns. Only `SELECT`, `WITH` "
"and `VALUES` statements run; anything else, such as `ATTACH`, is refused and "
"the exit code is 2."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:172
msgid "What you have done"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:174
msgid ""
"You checked a project and read the exit code, wrote its pages as PDF and "
"SVG, exported a cable list and a bill of materials, and queried the project "
"in SQL, all from the terminal. The same commands run in a script or a CI "
"job, because they read the project without locking it and never need a "
"display."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:178
msgid "Where to go next"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:180
msgid ""
"The [command reference](../reference/commands/index.md) lists the commands "
"that change a project, and the [rule reference](../reference/rules/index.md) "
"the rules that `ew check` runs."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:182
msgid ""
"More [how-to guides](index.md) for single tasks, such as checking a project "
"in a CI job, will follow this one."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/headless-workflow.md:183
msgid ""
"The [explanation](../explanation/index.md) section links the design of the "
"model and of the check engine."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide gives a drawn potential its name, its type and its voltage in the "
"application window, so that its wires take the colours of the project's "
"profile and the reports print its name. A potential that you have only drawn "
"is known by its connection points and has a fallback name until you define "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:9 src/how-to/choose-parts.md:9
msgid "A project open for writing, with a schematic page shown."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:10
msgid "A wire, a junction or a terminal on the potential."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:14
msgid "Select one item on the potential:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:15
msgid ""
"a wire: the potential is defined at its start, a connection point or a "
"junction;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:16
msgid "a junction;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:17
msgid "a terminal: the potential is defined at its first connection point."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:18
msgid "Open **Define potential…** in one of these ways:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:19
msgid "the **Draw** menu, under **Connect**, beside **Bond**;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:20
msgid "a right-click on the item, then **Define potential…**;"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:21
msgid ""
"the command palette (`Ctrl+K`): type `potential`, `supply` or `voltage`, "
"then press Enter."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:22
msgid ""
"In the form, type the **Name** (`24VDC-1`). The **Type** and the **Voltage** "
"may stay empty; choose the type from its list, the words Properties shows "
"(`L+`, `L-`, `L1`, `N`, `PE`, …), and type the voltage as the pages print "
"it: `24 V DC` or `230 V AC`. `DC` or `AC` in the voltage also sets the "
"current."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:25
msgid ""
"Press Enter. The potential is defined in one step: **Undo** takes it back."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:27
msgid ""
"When nothing, or more than one item, is selected, **Define potential…** is "
"greyed out and its tooltip says what to select. A name left empty is refused "
"with \"Name the potential first\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:30
msgid "Accept a reasoned potential"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:32
msgid ""
"A selected wire whose potential has no name yet may show a small card beside "
"it: \"Potential 0VDC?\" with the reason, such as \"-K1:A2 returns a coil fed "
"from 24VDC\". The window offers a name only where it is safe to reason: a "
"pin whose part or function definition gives its role (the − of a DC supply, "
"an N or PE pin), or the A2 of a coil whose A1 is on L+ (then A2 is the 0 V) "
"or on one phase (then A2 is the N). The name is the project's named "
"potential of that type; where there is none, the card offers a new one "
"(\"New potential 0V?\"). **Accept** defines it, as Define potential… would, "
"in one step; **Decline** hides the card for that wire until the project is "
"opened again. Nothing is set without your Accept."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:39
msgid "Check the result"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:41
msgid ""
"Open the **Potentials** navigator: the potential's row now carries the name "
"you typed, with its type and voltage. In the navigator's table view you can "
"also change them later in the **Potential**, **Type** and **Voltage** cells."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/define-supply-potential.md:44
msgid ""
"The command behind the form is "
"[`potential.define`](../reference/commands/potential.define.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide gives a device its part, the orderable article with its "
"manufacturer and order number, and shows where the project's parts are "
"listed. A device without a part is reported by the check EW-CORE-041 and is "
"missing from the parts list."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:10
msgid "The starter parts that come with EW, or a library of your own."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:12
msgid "Give a device its part"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:14
msgid "Click the device on the page, for example a lamp."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:15
msgid ""
"Choose **Edit ▸ Choose part…**. The property grid's **Parts** row offers the "
"same in its row menu."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:16
msgid ""
"In the part selection, type a few letters of the part, such as `lamp`, "
"choose the part (EW-GEN-LMP-1 for the starter lamp) and confirm with "
"**Choose**."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:19
msgid ""
"The device's **Parts** row now shows the part, and the warning EW-CORE-041 "
"for the device disappears from the Messages pane. **Edit ▸ Edit part…** "
"changes the project's copy of the part."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:22
msgid "Place a device with its part"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:24
msgid ""
"When you know the part before you draw, place the device from it, so that "
"the device has its part from the start:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:26
msgid "Choose **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Device from part…** (`Shift+I`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:27
msgid ""
"In the browser, double-click the part, for example the terminal "
"EW-GEN-TRM-1; its row places the part's main function. To place another "
"function, open the part with its arrow and double-click the function."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:29
msgid "Click where the device goes on the page."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:31
msgid "Read the parts list"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:33
msgid ""
"**Output ▸ Open parts list** opens the parts-list page of the project, when "
"it has one: a project made with the company standard wizard, and the sample "
"project, have one."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:35
msgid ""
"**View ▸ Panels ▸ Navigators ▸ Parts in use** lists the parts the project "
"uses with their devices, in any project."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/choose-parts.md:37
msgid ""
"The part data comes from the library: [Edit "
"part](../reference/window/dialogs.md#edit-part) changes the project's copy, "
"and the [glossary](../reference/glossary.md#part) relates EW's part to "
"EPLAN's article and QElectroTech's manufacturer reference."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:3
msgid ""
"A panel layout page shows an enclosure's mounting plate to scale, with its "
"rails, its wiring ducts and the devices placed on them. The devices are the "
"project's own: a device of the schematic is placed once, and the panel "
"layout, the parts list and the enclosure legend read it from there."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:7
msgid "Make the page"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:9
msgid ""
"Choose **Page ▸ New page…**, set **Type** to Panel layout, give the page a "
"title such as \"Panel layout\", and choose **Create**. The page opens empty; "
"the Draw tab's Rail and Duct say what is missing."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:11
msgid ""
"An enclosure belongs to a location (`+K1`). If the project has none yet, add "
"one with **Project ▸ Setup ▸ Structure node…**: kind Location, designation "
"`K1`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:14
msgid "Add the enclosure"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:16
msgid ""
"Choose **Project ▸ Setup ▸ Enclosure…**. The dialog proposes the page's "
"location and the first enclosure part of the project or of an installed "
"library, such as \"Wall enclosure 600 × 800 × 250 mm\"; choose another from "
"the lists if you like, then **Create**. The page now shows the enclosure and "
"its mounting plate, centred and at the scale that fits the page. Another "
"plate goes on the page through the page's **Surface** row in Properties."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:22
msgid "Rails and ducts"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:24
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Rail** and click the rail's start and end on the plate (a "
"rail runs across or up and down); the tool's card offers the rail part and "
"the grid step. Draw as many rails as the cabinet needs."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:26
msgid ""
"Choose **Draw ▸ Duct** and draw the ducts the same way; the card asks for a "
"duct's size when its part gives none."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:28
msgid "Place the devices"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:30
msgid ""
"Show **View ▸ Panels ▸ Navigators ▸ Unplaced devices**. It lists the devices "
"of the page's location that have no place yet. A device without a location "
"is not listed: give it the location in Properties, under Structure."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:32
msgid ""
"Drag a device from the list onto a rail, or select its row, press `Enter` "
"and click the place. On a rail it snaps to its neighbour, the rail's ends or "
"the plate grid; a device mounted by screws goes on the plate."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:34
msgid ""
"To fill a rail at once, select the rail and choose **Draw ▸ Auto-fill "
"rail…**: it places the devices you marked in the list, or every unplaced "
"device that fits, in the order of the schematic or of their tags, and a "
"toast says what did not fit."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/panel-layout.md:38
msgid ""
"The tools are described one by one in the [tool "
"reference](../reference/window/tools.md): Place device, Rail and Duct."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:3
msgid ""
"This guide takes a project made with an earlier release of ElektraWatt to "
"the release you have installed. Installing the new release itself is in "
"[Install ElektraWatt](install.md#upgrade)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:6
msgid "What a release reads and writes"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:8
msgid ""
"The version of ElektraWatt and the version of the project format are "
"numbered separately. Each release writes one format, the current one, and "
"reads that format and every older one it has a migration for. The [release "
"notes](../reference/release-notes/index.md) of each release name the format "
"it writes."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:12
msgid ""
"**An older format** opens migrated in memory: you see and edit the project "
"as the current format holds it, and nothing on disk changes until you save."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:14
msgid ""
"**A newer or an unreleased format**, one that a later release or a "
"development build wrote, opens read-only. The banner says so: \"This project "
"is open read-only: a file format this version does not write.\" Open it with "
"the release that wrote it."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:18
msgid "Upgrade in the window"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:20
msgid ""
"Open the project with **File ▸ Open…** (`Ctrl+O`). The banner says \"This "
"project is in an older format; saving converts it.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:22
msgid ""
"Choose **File ▸ Save** (`Ctrl+S`). The dialog \"Convert this project to the "
"current format?\" names the older format and the new one. Older versions of "
"ElektraWatt cannot open the project once it is converted."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:25
msgid ""
"Choose **Upgrade and save**. ElektraWatt first writes a backup of the "
"project as it was, `<project folder>.pre-<old format>.ewz`, beside the "
"project folder, then saves the project in the current format. If a file of "
"that name already holds something else, the backup takes the next free name "
"(`.pre-<old format>-2.ewz` and so on)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:30
msgid ""
"To go back, keep the backup: it is the whole project in one file. **File ▸ "
"Import…** opens it read-only, or extracts it into a new folder beside it, "
"which the earlier release opens."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:33
msgid "Upgrade from the terminal"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:35
msgid "`ew upgrade` does the same without the window:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:42
msgid ""
"`--check` only reports: it exits 0 when the project needs no upgrade and 1 "
"when it is in an older format, and it writes nothing."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:44
msgid ""
"Without `--check`, `ew upgrade` asks you to type `upgrade` to confirm, "
"writes the backup beside the project folder and saves the project. `--yes` "
"confirms without asking, for a script; without a terminal and without "
"`--yes` it changes nothing and exits 2."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:48
msgid ""
"The [CLI reference](../reference/cli/upgrade.md) lists every flag and exit "
"code."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:50
msgid "What changes in the format from 0.1.0"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to/upgrade-a-project.md:52
msgid ""
"Until the release 0.1.0 the format changed from build to build without "
"migrations. From 0.1.0 on, the format that 0.1.0 writes is fixed: a later "
"release changes it only by adding to it, so that a project of 0.1.0 opens "
"and saves unchanged, or by a new format version that comes with a migration "
"from the released one, and with the first save's backup described above. The "
"rule is the project's [format "
"decision](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/adr/0011-format-versioning-and-migration.md), "
"amendment 6."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:3
msgid ""
"The reference states exact facts about EW and nothing else: what a command "
"takes, what a rule checks, what a program prints. Look here when you know "
"what you are doing and need the detail. To learn, use a "
"[tutorial](../tutorials/index.md); to understand the design, read the "
"[explanation](../explanation/index.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:7
msgid "Generated reference"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:9
msgid ""
"These pages are generated from the code by `cargo xtask docs-gen` and "
"checked in CI, so they cannot fall behind it (NFR-DOC-06). Do not edit them "
"by hand."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:12
msgid ""
"[Command reference](commands/index.md): every registered command, with its "
"parameters, types, constraints and exit codes, from the parameter schemas of "
"the command registry."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:14
msgid ""
"[Rule reference](rules/index.md): every shipped check rule, one page per "
"rule with an index page per pack, from the rule registry and the rule notes."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:16
msgid ""
"[CLI reference](cli/index.md): every subcommand of `ew`, with its flags, "
"exit codes and `--json` result schema, from the CLI table."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:18
msgid ""
"[Query reference](queries.md): every public query of `ew query`, with its "
"key, example and output, from the query registry."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:20
msgid ""
"[api_v1 reference](api-v1.md): every `api_v1` view of `ew sql`, with its "
"columns, types and Preview marks."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:21
msgid ""
"[Format reference](format/index.md): every record kind of the project "
"format, with its file, rank and fields, from the format's JSON Schemas."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:23
msgid ""
"[Release notes](release-notes/index.md): the release and migration notes of "
"every release, newest first, from the notes in the repository."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:26
msgid "The window"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:28
msgid ""
"What each part of the desktop application's window does and how to open it. "
"Help ▸ Documentation opens the section of the tool, dialog or pane you are "
"in."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:31
msgid "[Tools](window/tools.md): every tool of the Draw tab."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:32
msgid "[Dialogs](window/dialogs.md): every dialog."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:33
msgid ""
"[Panels](window/panels.md): every pane and navigator, the strip editor and "
"the table mode."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:35
msgid "Command-line program"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:37
msgid ""
"The usage text of `ew` is part of the program: `ew --help` lists the "
"subcommands and their options, and `ew sql schema` prints the `api_v1` views "
"that `ew sql` queries. The authoritative design catalogue, with the exit "
"codes of every subcommand, is [doc 17 "
"§3](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/design/17-extensibility.md#3-the-ew-cli)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:42
msgid "Not generated yet"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/index.md:44
msgid ""
"The documentation of the plugin interface (WIT, 1.x) is also meant to be "
"generated (NFR-DOC-06). It is not part of the book yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:3
msgid ""
"A tool decides what a click on the page does. One tool is active at a time; "
"Select is active when no other is. The Draw tab of the command bar holds the "
"tools, and each is in the Draw menu at the path given below. Help ▸ "
"Documentation with a tool active opens its section here."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:7
msgid ""
"The keys of every tool are on the [keymap](../keymaps.md) page, for each "
"keymap preset. A tool's own keys work only while it is active. `Esc` or a "
"right click on the page cancels what the active tool is doing, one step at a "
"time, and ends at Select; with Select active, a right click opens the page's "
"menu."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:11
msgid ""
"The page's menu is the quick way to what fits the thing under the pointer: "
"its first entries are that thing's, then come the general ones (Rotate, "
"Mirror and Renumber devices… on a symbol; Cut, Copy, Paste, Delete, "
"Properties of selection). A right click selects the thing first; Shift+F10 "
"or the menu key opens the same menu for the selection."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:15
msgid ""
"On a wire: Edit route, Wire marker, Define potential…, Cable… and Print wire "
"numbers; a wire does not turn, so there is no Rotate or Mirror. On several "
"selected wires: Make cable from the selection…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:17
msgid ""
"On a terminal: Add terminal (the strip's next number, floating at the "
"pointer) and Edit strip, which opens the strip editor. A strip's row in the "
"Terminal strips navigator offers the same two."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:19
msgid ""
"On a relay's coil: Add contact, one entry per free contact of the relay by "
"name; it floats the contact's symbol for a click. On a contact: Go to coil."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:21
msgid ""
"On a symbol of a device: Join device… (only where another device can take "
"it, such as a contact another relay has free), Choose part…, Edit symbol and "
"Copy and edit."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:23
msgid ""
"On an interruption point: Supply comes in here…, Go to partner, Direction: "
"Source or Target, and Continue on page…. Supply comes in here… says that the "
"supply enters here from another drawing set (L1, L2, L3, N, PE from another "
"drawing): its form proposes where the supply comes from and the device "
"there, and one Apply marks every selected point; the points then print where "
"they come from and are no longer reported as unpaired. On a marked point the "
"entry reads Remove the supply mark. The same link stands under the point's "
"name in Properties."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:28
msgid ""
"On the title block: Title block fields…. On an empty spot: Paste, Page "
"properties, New page… and Print wire numbers."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:30
msgid ""
"A navigator's row offers what the same thing offers on the page; a right "
"click on a row selects it first. A device's row offers Go to (its first "
"placement), its symbol's entries and Properties of selection; a cable's row "
"Cable… and Go to; a page's row Open page in a new tab, Title block fields…, "
"Page properties, New page… and Delete page (the right click opens the page). "
"See [Navigators](panels.md#navigators)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:35
msgid "An entry that cannot be used now is greyed out, and its tooltip says why."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:37
msgid "Select"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:39
msgid ""
"Selects, moves and opens what you click: a click selects one item, a drag "
"draws a selection band, a double click opens the item's properties. Open it "
"with Draw ▸ Select. See [Use the application "
"window](../../how-to/application-window.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:43
msgid "Measure"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:45
msgid ""
"Measures the distance between two points on the page, in millimetres of the "
"sheet. Open it with Draw ▸ Annotate ▸ Measure."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:48 src/reference/symbol-editor.md:169
msgid "Symbol"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:50
msgid ""
"Places a symbol from the library. A symbol is a drawing without a part; the "
"symbol browser lists the symbols alone (the parts are the device browser's, "
"the terminals Terminal's). Double-click the symbol in the browser (its "
"categories at the left, a picture of the chosen row at the right), then "
"click where it goes; each click places one more until you finish. Under the "
"picture, **Edit symbol** opens the symbol in the symbol editor, where its "
"next version is saved to its library; a symbol of a read-only library (the "
"starter's) or one only the project holds offers **Copy and edit** alone, "
"which opens a copy that is saved to your own library. Open it with Draw ▸ "
"Place ▸ Symbol…. See [A first schematic page](../../tutorials/first-page.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:58
msgid "Device from part"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:60
msgid ""
"Places a device from a part of the library, so that the device has its part "
"from the start. A device is a part of the catalogue with its functions. The "
"browser lists one row per part; a part with several functions opens to them "
"with its arrow (or Right). Edit symbol and Copy and edit act on the symbol "
"of the chosen row, as in the symbol browser. Open it with Draw ▸ Place ▸ "
"Device from part…. See [Choose parts](../../how-to/choose-parts.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:65
msgid "Terminal"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:67
msgid ""
"Asks which strip (one of the project's, or a new strip with the tag it will "
"take), which kind of terminal (feed-through, protective earth and the others "
"of the page's library), how many and from which number (empty: the strip's "
"next free number). Place then floats the first terminal; one click lays the "
"whole row, each terminal two grid steps right of the one before. Draw ▸ "
"Place ▸ Terminal…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:72 src/explanation/eplan-and-ew.md:112
msgid "Black box"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:74
msgid ""
"Draws a black box: a rectangle with connection points for a device that the "
"library has no symbol for. Open it with Draw ▸ Place ▸ Black box…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:77
msgid "Tool step back"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:79
msgid ""
"Takes back the last step of the active tool, such as the last corner of a "
"wire, without leaving the tool. It has no menu item; use its key."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:82
msgid "Rotate clockwise"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:84
msgid ""
"Turns the symbol that the tool floats, or the selection, by 90° clockwise. "
"Draw ▸ Arrange ▸ Rotate clockwise."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:86
msgid "Rotate counter-clockwise"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:88
msgid ""
"Turns the floating symbol, or the selection, by 90° counter-clockwise. Draw "
"▸ Arrange ▸ Rotate counter-clockwise."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:90
msgid "Mirror left to right"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:92
msgid ""
"Mirrors the floating symbol, or the selection, about its vertical axis. Draw "
"▸ Arrange ▸ Mirror left to right."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:94
msgid "Mirror top to bottom"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:96
msgid ""
"Mirrors the floating symbol, or the selection, about its horizontal axis. "
"Draw ▸ Arrange ▸ Mirror top to bottom."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:98
msgid "Next variant"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:100
msgid ""
"Switches the floating symbol to its next variant, such as another contact "
"arrangement of the same function. Draw ▸ Arrange ▸ Next variant."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:103
msgid "Next representation type"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:105
msgid ""
"Switches the floating symbol to its next representation type, for example "
"from the multi-line to the single-line form. Draw ▸ Arrange ▸ Next "
"representation type."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:108
msgid "Move"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:110
msgid ""
"Moves the selection with the pointer or the keyboard; wires attached to it "
"follow. Draw ▸ Arrange ▸ Move."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:112
msgid ""
"When you let go, a wire whose followed route would now run through a symbol, "
"a text, a connection point or along another wire, or would bend more than "
"before, is routed again; a wire that stays clean keeps its shape. Both "
"happen in the same undo step as the move."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:116
msgid "Finish"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:118
msgid ""
"Ends what the active tool is drawing, such as a wire or a polyline, at the "
"current point. It has no menu item; use its key or double-click where a tool "
"allows it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:121
msgid "Wire"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:123
msgid ""
"Draws connections between connection points: click a start point, then the "
"end point; corners are placed where you click in between. Open it with Draw "
"▸ Connect ▸ Wire. See [A first schematic "
"page](../../tutorials/first-page.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:126
msgid ""
"While you draw, the wire shows the route it will take. The route never runs "
"through another connection point, along another wire, through a symbol or a "
"text, over the frame's bands, or through a junction, and never makes a "
"fourth arm of a junction. When no such route exists the wire shows as a "
"dashed right-angled line and the hint says \"No clear route: the wire stays "
"unrouted. Click to place bends.\" Ending it there stores an unrouted wire: "
"it keeps its connection, but it is drawn dashed and the check reports it "
"until it has a route. Click on empty points to place bends and the wire "
"follows them (\"Bend placed: click a connection point to end the wire.\"). A "
"junction that already joins three wires is not an end: the hint says \"This "
"junction already joins three wires: end on a wire to make a new junction.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:135
msgid ""
"An unrouted wire, from an import or a placement, is drawn as a dashed line "
"between its ends, straight or with one corner and never diagonal, on the "
"page and in the PDF. The line takes no notice of what lies under it: it is "
"no route yet. Route page or Route selection draws it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:139
msgid "Next autoconnect proposal"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:141
msgid ""
"While a symbol floats near other connection points, shows the next of the "
"connections EW proposes to draw when you place it. It has no menu item; use "
"its key."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:144
msgid "Connect while moving"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:146
msgid ""
"While moving a symbol, connects its connection points to the ones it lands "
"on. It has no menu item; use its key."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:148
msgid ""
"Connect while moving is on when you start: the wires the moved symbol would "
"make are shown while you drag and are drawn with the move when you let go, "
"in the same undo step. Hold the key that places without wires as you let go "
"to move without them. The key of Connect while moving turns it off, and on "
"again, until you close the program; the hint of the move and the status bar "
"show the key followed by \"Connect on move: on\" or \"off\". A move with the "
"keyboard follows that setting."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:154
msgid "Edit route"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:156
msgid ""
"Changes the drawn route of a wire with the mouse. Select the wire: a handle "
"shows on each of its segments and a dashed one on each bend. Drag a segment "
"and it moves sideways; drag a bend and the corner moves. The wire stays "
"right-angled and its ends stay on their connection points: a first or last "
"segment that is dragged keeps a short stub at its end and jogs after it, so "
"a straight wire dragged sideways goes round what was in its way. A dashed "
"line shows the new route while you drag, and one undo takes the change back. "
"A wire without a route, drawn dashed, takes its first route this way too. "
"Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Edit route does the same from the keyboard: `Tab` chooses "
"the segment, the arrow keys move it, `Enter` stores it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:164
msgid "Route page"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:166
msgid ""
"Routes every unrouted wire of the page on show in one step. Open it with "
"Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Route page. The Preview tab shows the plan first: \"Route 3 "
"wires on folio 101: 2 routed, 1 not routed.\", and for each wire that no "
"route keeps clear, why, such as \"No route keeps clear of other wires: "
"-101F1:2 → -101E55:L.\" Apply draws the routes, and one Undo takes them "
"back; the toast says \"Routed 2 wires on folio 101.\" When every wire of the "
"page has a route it says \"Every wire on folio 101 has a route.\" instead. "
"The router routes supply wires and long wires first, keeps the wires of a "
"feed side by side in the order L1, L2, L3, N, PE where their ends allow it, "
"and keeps every route clear as the Wire tool does. A wire it cannot route "
"stays unrouted; move a symbol to make room, or place its bends with the Wire "
"tool."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:175
msgid "Route selection"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:177
msgid ""
"Routes the selected wires, and every wire with an end on a selected symbol, "
"again, routed or not; the other wires of the page stay as they are. Open it "
"with Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Route selection. It shows its plan and applies it as "
"Route page does."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:181 src/explanation/eplan-and-ew.md:44
msgid "Interruption point"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:183
msgid ""
"Places an interruption point: the end of a potential on this page that "
"continues at a point of the same name elsewhere. Open it with Draw ▸ Connect "
"▸ Interruption point."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:186
msgid ""
"The arrow always points right, the way the signal flows. A source, where the "
"potential leaves the page, has its wire on the arrow's tail and belongs at "
"the page's right edge; a target, where the potential arrives, has its wire "
"on the arrow's tip and belongs at the left edge. The name is above the arrow "
"and the cross-reference below it. The arrow is never rotated or mirrored: "
"change the point's direction instead. While you draw a wire, the "
"interruption point's key ends the wire in a point of the right form for that "
"end."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:192
msgid ""
"Type the name in the field Interruption point name at the page's top left, "
"once for a pair: the first point of a name is its source, and the tool then "
"floats the same name as its target, on this page or the next. After the "
"target the name is used up, and each further point gets a name of its own "
"until you type one. When you choose the tool from the Draw tab or the menu, "
"the field has the keys: type the name and press Enter, then click where the "
"point goes."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:197
msgid ""
"A point keeps room for its texts: where its name or its cross-reference "
"would print over another interruption point's, it is not placed, and the "
"message says so. Three grid steps below or above the other point is clear; "
"points side by side are clear when their texts are. Hold Alt to place it "
"anyway."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:201
msgid "Interruption point direction"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:203
msgid ""
"Turns the interruption point that the tool floats from source to target, or "
"back. Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Interruption point direction. An undirected point, "
"which pairs either way, is a choice of a placed point's Direction in its "
"properties."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:207
msgid ""
"The point takes the form of its new direction: the wire moves from the tail "
"to the tip, or back. Changing the direction of a placed point in its "
"properties does the same, and is refused when the project does not hold the "
"other form yet: place one point of that direction first."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:211
msgid "Star source"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:213
msgid ""
"Marks the interruption point as the source of its name in star mode: one "
"source, many targets. Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Star source."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:216
msgid "Wire marker"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:218
msgid ""
"Places a wire marker, the label that prints the cross-section and colours of "
"the wires it marks. Open it with **Marker** on the Draw tab or Shift+W. Drag "
"a line across the wires, type the cross-section and the colours in the "
"tool's fields if they are to change, and press Enter. The fields may be "
"filled before or after the line: a field takes its value when you leave it "
"(Enter, Tab, a click on the other field or back on the page), and the marker "
"prints what the fields show. A colour is a code in any case (`BK`, `bk`), "
"two codes of one wire (`GN+YE` or `GNYE`), a company code of the project's "
"colour profile, or a colour's name from that profile; one colour is every "
"marked wire's, and a list (`BK, BU`) gives each wire its own in the order "
"the line crosses them. A colour the project does not know is refused with "
"the codes it knows. The marker prints a short line across the wires and its "
"text beyond the line's end, clear of the wires."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:228
msgid ""
"The wires' numbers print on the page with **Draw ▸ Connect ▸ Print wire "
"numbers**, a setting of the project that the PDF and print follow too: each "
"wire's number stands beside it, once, at the middle of its longest segment, "
"and on a wire that carries its own marker the number comes first in the "
"marker's text (`101.1 1.5 BK`). A cable's core prints no number; its cable "
"and core name it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:233
msgid "Text"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:235
msgid "Places a free text on the page. Open it with Draw ▸ Annotate ▸ Text."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:237
msgid "Graphic"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:239
msgid ""
"Draws a graphic: a line, polyline, rectangle, circle, arc or polygon, which "
"has no electrical meaning. Open it with Draw ▸ Annotate ▸ Graphic and the "
"shape."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:242 src/explanation/eplan-and-ew.md:120
msgid "Structure box"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:244
msgid ""
"Draws a structure box: a dashed frame that puts what it encloses into a "
"function or location of the project's structure. Open it with Draw ▸ "
"Annotate ▸ Structure box."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:247
msgid "Image"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:249
msgid ""
"Places an image file (SVG, PNG or JPEG) on the page. Open it with Draw ▸ "
"Annotate ▸ Image…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:251
msgid "Place device"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:253
msgid ""
"On a panel layout page, places a device of the location on a rail or the "
"mounting plate. Open it from the Unplaced devices navigator, which lists the "
"devices of the location that have no place yet."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:256
msgid "Rail"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:258
msgid ""
"On a panel layout page, draws a mounting rail. Open it with Draw ▸ Rail. The "
"page shows an enclosure first: Project ▸ Setup ▸ Enclosure… puts the new "
"enclosure's plate on it ([Draw a panel "
"layout](../../how-to/panel-layout.md))."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:261
msgid "Duct"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/tools.md:263
msgid "On a panel layout page, draws a wiring duct. Open it with Draw ▸ Duct."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:3
msgid ""
"A dialog asks for a decision or a few values and closes when you answer. "
"Help ▸ Documentation in an open dialog opens its section, here or in [Use "
"the application window](../../how-to/application-window.md), which describes "
"these dialogs:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:7
msgid ""
"About ElektraWatt and the third-party notices: "
"[About](../../how-to/application-window.md#about);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:8
msgid ""
"the keyboard shortcuts: [Keyboard "
"shortcuts](../../how-to/application-window.md#keyboard-shortcuts);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:9
msgid ""
"Save, Discard or Cancel before a change would be lost, and the recovery of "
"unsaved changes: [Unsaved changes and "
"recovery](../../how-to/application-window.md#unsaved-changes-and-recovery);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:11
msgid ""
"the check scheme the company standard wizard applies: [Company standard "
"wizard](../../how-to/application-window.md#company-standard-wizard);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:13
msgid ""
"the name of a saved layout: "
"[Workspaces](../../how-to/application-window.md#workspaces);"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:14
msgid ""
"the QElectroTech import: [Import a QElectroTech "
"project](../../how-to/application-window.md#import-a-qelectrotech-project)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:16
msgid "The keys of a dialog's buttons are on the [keymap](../keymaps.md) page."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:18
msgid "Go to page or cell"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:20
msgid ""
"Opens a page by its folio, or a cell of a page by its folio and grid "
"reference, such as 102-B4. Open it with Page ▸ Go ▸ Go to page or cell…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:23
msgid "Form dialogs"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:25
msgid ""
"The fields of an action that needs values before it runs, such as Page ▸ New "
"page…, Project ▸ Setup ▸ Structure node… and Project ▸ Setup ▸ External "
"reference…. The primary button runs the action with the values; Cancel "
"changes nothing."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:29
msgid ""
"Page ▸ Title block fields…, also a link under the page's name in Properties, "
"lists every field the page's title block prints from the project's data, in "
"the title block's order: the page's title and description, the project "
"reference, the drawing and object IDs, the dates, the parties' names and the "
"revision. Apply writes each changed field where its value comes from in one "
"undo step: the description to the page when the page overrides it, else to "
"the project; a revision label typed in a project without a revision makes "
"that revision. Author and Checked are the names of the parties the current "
"revision names as its author and its checker: the form edits those names. An "
"open revision without an author takes the name typed as its author's (Set "
"revision author's step); Checked has a field only once the revision names "
"its checker, who is set when it is approved, and the form says so. A text "
"the title block's form holds itself (the fixed texts of an imported "
"QElectroTech template) is not a field, and the form says so under its fields."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:40
msgid "Purge unused images"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:42
msgid ""
"Lists the image files that no page uses any more and deletes them on Purge. "
"Undo does not bring them back. Open it with Project ▸ Purge unused images…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:45
msgid "Choose part"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:47
msgid ""
"The part selection: search the library, filter the results and choose the "
"part for the selected devices. Open it with Edit ▸ Choose part…. See [Choose "
"parts](../../how-to/choose-parts.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:50
msgid "Edit part"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:52
msgid ""
"The part editor: change the members of the project's copy of a part and save "
"them. Open it with Edit ▸ Edit part…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:54
msgid "Print"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:56
msgid ""
"The print options beside a preview of the print set; Print… hands the set to "
"the printer, Open in PDF viewer opens it there. Open it with Output ▸ "
"Print…. See [Reports and PDF](../../tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:59
msgid "Changes on disk"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:61
msgid ""
"The project changed on disk while you have unsaved edits to the same fields: "
"each colliding field with its base, your value and the value on disk. Choose "
"a side per field and Apply, take all of one side, or decide later. It opens "
"by itself; File ▸ Check for changes on disk looks again."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:65
msgid "Upgrade the format"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:67
msgid ""
"The first save of a project in an older format: Upgrade and save writes a "
"backup beside the project, then saves in the current format; Cancel saves "
"nothing. It opens by itself. See [Upgrade a "
"project](../../how-to/upgrade-a-project.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:70
msgid "Re-import a matched file"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:72
msgid ""
"A QElectroTech file that matches an earlier import of this project by its "
"items: Re-import compares it with that import; Cancel stops. Open it with "
"Project ▸ Re-import QElectroTech project…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:75
msgid "Re-import QElectroTech project"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:77
msgid ""
"The re-import card: the records the source added, changed, changed on both "
"sides and removed, in words. Apply takes them over as one undo step; Cancel "
"changes nothing. The migration report then lists what needs a decision."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:80
msgid "Open .ewz"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:82
msgid ""
"An `.ewz` archive of a project: Extract and open unpacks it into a new "
"folder beside it, Open read-only opens it without unpacking. Open it with "
"File ▸ Import…, which takes a QElectroTech project as well."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:85
msgid "Set up merge driver"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/dialogs.md:87
msgid ""
"Sets up the repository's git configuration so that git merges the project's "
"files record by record. It asks first and writes nothing on Cancel. Open it "
"with Project ▸ Set up merge driver."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:3
msgid ""
"The window's panes sit around the page view: the navigators on the left, the "
"property grid on the right, and the find pane, the jobs, the preview, the "
"history and the glossary in the bottom dock. View ▸ Panels shows and hides "
"them. Help ▸ Documentation in a focused pane opens its section here. The "
"keys of each pane are on the [keymap](../keymaps.md) page."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:8
msgid "Panes"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:10
msgid "Page view"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:12
msgid ""
"The canvas: the open pages as tabs, one or two groups side by side. The "
"tools of the Draw tab act on it; see [Tools](tools.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:15
msgid "Properties"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:17
msgid ""
"The property grid: the members of the selection, by category, each editable "
"in place. A click on a category's header, or Enter on it, folds the "
"category. The pointer resting on a row's label explains the row. A derived "
"value's chip names where it comes from, such as the profile or the "
"potential. A row's menu holds the actions of that member, such as Choose "
"part on the Parts row, Go to source on a derived value, and Mark not used on "
"a device's connection point. A wire and an interruption point show their "
"potential's definition, where its type and voltage are set, or Define "
"potential… when it has none. View ▸ Panels ▸ Properties shows it; Edit ▸ "
"Properties of selection moves the focus to it. See [Choose "
"parts](../../how-to/choose-parts.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:25
msgid "Find"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:27
msgid ""
"The find pane: search texts, tags and fields of the project, and replace "
"them. Open it with Edit ▸ Find… or Edit ▸ Find and replace…."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:30
msgid "Jobs"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:32
msgid ""
"The jobs: the PDF exports that run in the background, with their progress "
"and results. Open it with Output ▸ Jobs. See [Reports and "
"PDF](../../tutorials/reports-and-pdf.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:35
msgid "Preview"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:37
msgid ""
"The preview of the open card: what a command, a quick fix or a paste would "
"change, before you apply it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:39
msgid "History"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:41
msgid ""
"The undo steps of the project and the marker lines of exports and "
"recoveries; undo to any step from here. Open it with View ▸ Panels ▸ History."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:44
msgid "Glossary"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:46
msgid ""
"The terms of EW with their EPLAN and QElectroTech names. Open it with Help ▸ "
"Glossary. See [EPLAN and EW](../../explanation/eplan-and-ew.md) and "
"[QElectroTech and EW](../../explanation/qet-and-ew.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:49
msgid "Command bar"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:51
msgid ""
"The tabs and groups of commands at the top of the window: Project, Page, "
"Draw, Check, Output and View. View ▸ Layout ▸ Collapse command bar folds it "
"to its tab row."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:54
msgid "Strip editor"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:56
msgid ""
"The Terminal strips navigator in table mode for one strip: insert, delete "
"and move terminals, add accessories and edit the cells of the terminal rows. "
"Open it with Edit ▸ Strip editor with a strip selected. See [A terminal "
"strip and a cable](../../tutorials/terminal-strip-and-cable.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:60
msgid "Table mode"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:62
msgid ""
"A navigator shown as a table: one row per record and a column per member, "
"edited cell by cell; fill down, pattern fill and paste work on several cells "
"at once. The navigator's Table button switches it. Output ▸ Export table… "
"and Output ▸ Import table… exchange the table with a spreadsheet."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:66
msgid "Navigators"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:68
msgid ""
"A navigator lists one kind of record of the project. View ▸ Panels ▸ "
"Navigators shows each one; clicking a row selects the record and opens its "
"page. A right click on a row selects it and opens its menu: what the same "
"thing offers on the page, listed below for each navigator."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:72
msgid "Pages"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:74
msgid "The pages in folio order, flat, by section or by structure."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:76
msgid ""
"The two buttons beside the filter make a page and a section; a right click "
"on a section offers the same. A right click on a page opens it and offers "
"Open page in a new tab, Title block fields…, Page properties, New page… and "
"Delete page. A section is a range of folios with a title, such as 100 to "
"199: New section proposes the next number and the next hundred, and the tree "
"lists the section at once, still empty. With a section chosen in the tree, "
"New page proposes its first free folio."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:82
msgid "Devices"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:84
msgid ""
"The devices with their functions and representations. A right click on a "
"device offers Go to (its first placement, such as `201-E4`), what its symbol "
"offers on the page (Add contact on a relay or a contactor, Choose part…, "
"Edit symbol, Copy and edit) and Properties of selection."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:88
msgid "Connections"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:90
msgid "The connections, the wires drawn on the pages."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:94
msgid ""
"The terminal strips with their terminals. Its table mode is the strip editor."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:96
msgid "Cables"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/window/panels.md:98
msgid ""
"The cables with their cores. A right click on a cable offers Cable…, which "
