#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:3
msgid ""
"Open **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol editor…**. A symbol draft has its own undo "
"history, drawing, function definition, connection points, text slots and "
"cross-reference anchor. Changes do not alter existing placements. Choose A "
"to draw; B–H show live rotated/mirrored previews. Select a graphic to move "
"it by dragging or edit its coordinates in **Drawing**. **Draw by "
"coordinates** also creates geometry from the keyboard. Pin coordinates, "
"directions and function mappings are editable in **Electrical data**. In "
"**Pin mapping**, drag a function pin onto a connection point or use its "
"list. Assigning a pin already used by another compatible connection swaps "
"their mappings in one undo step. Repeated designations have an ordinal in "
"parentheses, so two terminals both named N remain distinguishable. Function "
"choices include their category and source library. In **Library**, **Fork "
"existing symbol** searches project and installed-library symbols by name or "
"key; each choice shows its source and grid. **Create editable copy** leaves "
"the original unchanged. The command bar and status bar follow the active "
"symbol; library paths and publication settings live in **Library**. The "
"ribbon uses the same small icons, labels and selected state as the main "
"window; each tool's tooltip says what it does and how (\"Line: drag, or "
"click two points\"). **More shapes** contains ellipse, arc, polyline and "
"polygon. Press **F1** in the editor to open this reference. Text fields can "
"show tags, function text, technical data, pin designations or "
"cross-references. **Advanced document** exposes the complete draft, "
"including other slot types, styles and arrangements. The circle tool takes a "
"centre and a point on its circumference; arcs and ellipses take opposite "
"bounding-box corners. Arc creation angles are whole degrees; negative sweep "
"reverses direction. A line, a rectangle, a circle, an ellipse and an arc are "
"drawn by two clicks or by one drag. With **Select**, click a shape, a "
"connection point, a text slot or a link point to select it; drag it or press "
"an arrow key to move it, and press **Delete** to remove it. While you drag, "
"an outline shows where the item will land. A connection point moves on the "
"connection grid; a text slot, a shape and a link point move by the drawing "
"grid's step. Each change takes one undo step. Numeric property edits commit "
"when you press Enter or leave the field, so partially typed coordinates do "
"not reshape the drawing. Selected rectangles, circles, ellipses and polygons "
"have **Fill shape**. Text and text slots have a **Text style** list showing "
"the project's style sizes; choose a larger style when connection numbers "
"need to match other labels."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:30
msgid ""
"**Anchor** sets the point used to calculate the symbol's cross-reference "
"cell on the project page. It does not move the symbol's insertion origin. "
"Place it on the left edge for a supply block. **Link point** instead adds a "
"named attachment position for a mechanical link; it does not create an "
"electrical connection."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:34
msgid ""
"Native connections must lie on the 4 mm grid. In the editor's header, beside "
"**Fit drawing**, **Drawing grid** chooses the step that lines, shapes, texts "
"and link points snap to (0.5, 1, 2 or 4 mm, or QET 10 px; 0.5 mm at first), "
"and **Snap to grid** turns that snapping off. The canvas draws the drawing "
"grid's dots and, larger, the connection grid's. A connection point keeps to "
"the connection grid whatever is chosen. The choice stays for the session."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:40
msgid ""
"**Category**, under the name on the Drawing and Library tabs, is where the "
"symbol browser and the device browser list the symbol, in their words "
"(\"Relays\", \"Power supplies\"); a new symbol starts under \"Assemblies and "
"black boxes\". A symbol with an assigned library function takes that "
"function's category. Numeric pin edits remain exactly as entered: a pin at "
"4.5 mm is visibly invalid and blocks publication. See the [design "
"standard](https://github.com/Ironkode787/ElektraWatt/blob/main/docs/design/07a-symbol-design-standard.md) "
"for the full authoring profile."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:48
msgid ""
"**Save to library** writes a symbol and its owned function definition to the "
"chosen `.ewlib` folder. The window remembers that folder. Its symbols appear "
"under **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol…** (`I`), with the library name; the page must "
"use the symbol's connection grid. Already embedded copies stay at their "
"existing version until you explicitly update them. Repeated placement keeps "
"the authored outline and pins. An assigned existing function definition "
"stays referenced and its semantics cannot be edited in place; any later "
"compatible version of it is accepted. A new symbol takes its key from its "
"name (`user.power-block` for **Power block**), and the key follows the name "
"until you type one in **Library**. The first save gives the draft its own "
"identity, and later saves of that draft update the same symbol: changed "
"content is saved as the next patch version (1.0.1 after 1.0.0), and a "
"version you type below the library's is refused. The symbol's own function "
"definition keeps its version while it is unchanged. A library that already "
"holds a key you typed for a different symbol refuses the save: choose "
"another key, or fork that symbol under its own key to change it; a key made "
"from the name takes the next free number instead (`user.power-block-2`). A "
"save says where the symbol went, and a refused one says why under the "
"symbol's name. Saving a draft under another key than its symbol's makes a "
"new symbol, and a message says so; the symbol with the earlier key stays. "
"**Export draft** writes portable JSON to a new file, including incomplete "
"work; an existing file is never overwritten. Leave **Symbol file** empty to "
"choose the file in a file dialog, and choose **Choose folder…** to pick the "
"library folder; an empty **Library folder** is refused when you save. A "
"relative path is read from your default user library folder. **Close "
"editor** keeps the draft and its undo history: **Draw ▸ Place ▸ Symbol "
"editor…** returns to it. Only **Discard draft** drops it, and **New symbol** "
"starts a fresh draft. While the draft has unsaved changes, **New symbol**, "
"**Open or import**, **Create editable copy**, another drawing of an import "
"and **Discard draft** first ask whether to save the symbol. While the editor "
"shows, Ctrl+S saves the symbol and the File menu's **Save project** saves "
"the project. Quitting with unsaved symbol changes asks whether to save the "
"symbol. **Test on a new page** creates a page in the current project with "
"the symbol's connection grid and places embedded copies there. The page then "
"shows with the copy selected; the message's **Back to the editor** returns "
"to the draft. Project undo removes the placement; a second undo removes the "
"new page. Test placements are independent draft copies, so **Source "
"missing** in the Libraries navigator refers to their temporary draft source. "
"To place a copy linked to the published library, use **Draw ▸ Place ▸ "
"Symbol…** after saving."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:78
msgid "AI and command-line tools"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:80
msgid "No remote service or generated code is required. Use `ew symbol`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:82
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"ew symbol template example.power \"Power block\" > power.symbol.json\n"
"ew symbol schema > symbol-authoring.schema.json\n"
"ew symbol validate power.symbol.json\n"
"ew symbol publish power.symbol.json --library symbols.ewlib\n"
"ew symbol import element.elmt --key example.import > imported-drafts.json\n"
"ew symbol import manufacturer.edz --key example.import > "
"imported-drafts.json\n"
"ew symbol import custom.slk --key example.import > imported-drafts.json\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:92
msgid ""
"Template and schema print JSON. Validation prints stable issue codes, JSON "
"pointers and details, exiting 0 when valid, 1 for validation findings, or 2 "
"for unreadable/malformed input. Import prints an **array** of drafts; choose "
"one object and save it as a `.symbol.json` file. QET conversion findings are "
"retained in `import_details` with their original message, code and source "
"line, so tools can identify each loss. Publication validates again and uses "
"the ordinary library writer's lock, version and transaction protections. It "
"does not save the current project. Without `--key`, import uses the file's "
"name as the key. Publication prints `result` (`added`, `updated` or "
"`unchanged`) and the draft's `identity`; copy that `identity` into the draft "
"file to publish later versions of the same symbol. A draft without it is a "
"new symbol, refused (exit 2) when the library holds its key for another one. "
"Validation reports a member it does not know as `symbol-unknown-member`, "
"which blocks publication."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/symbol-editor.md:102
msgid ""
"The portable envelope uses `format: \"ew.symbol/1\"`, `key`, `version`, "
"`symbol`, `function`, optional `identity`, `function_ref`, `import_notes`, "
"`import_details` and `import_reviewed`. File coordinates are integer "
"**nanometres**; 4 mm is `4000000`. A pin has `idx`, `fn_cp`, `pos: [x,y]` "
"and outward `dir`. The function's pin with the corresponding `idx` holds its "
"printed `designation`. Keep the reference created by the template; the "
"publisher replaces temporary references for owned functions."
msgstr ""

